# Use of breakthrough experiment to evaluate the performance of hydrogen isotope separation for metal-organic frameworks M-MOF-74 (M=Co, Ni, Mg, Zn)

Use of breakthrough experiment to evaluate the performance of hydrogen isotope separation for metal-organic frameworks M-MOF-74 $\bf{(M\mathrm{{=}}C\mathbf{0}}$ , Ni, Mg, Zn)
Yanan Si1,2, Wenjing Wang1, El-Sayed M. El-Sayed1,2,3 & Daqiang Yuan1
1State Key Laboratory of Structural Chemistry, Fujian Institute of Research on the Structure of Matter, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Fuzhou 350002, China; 2University of the Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing 100049, China; 3Chemical Refining Laboratory, Refining Department, Egyptian Petroleum Research Institute, Nasr City, Cairo, Egypt
The unique adsorption performance of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) indicates a new direction for gas separation and purification. The low-temperature distillation, as a traditional technique for hydrogen isotope separation, is limited as it is a high energy- and cost-intensive process. Instead of utilizing such a conventional separation route, we use ordered microporous frameworks based on a physical adsorption mechanism to solve the challenge of hydrogen isotope separation. Herein we analyze M-MOF-74 $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{}0$ , Ni, Mg, Zn), featuring a hexagonal channel about $11\mathrm{\AA}$ and high density of open metal sites, for their ability to separate and purify deuterium from the hydrogen isotope mixture by dynamic column breakthrough experiments. Our results show that the combination of the strength of binding sites, density of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites and hydrogen isotope adsorption capacity of materials renders Co-MOF-74 as an optimal adsorbent for the capture of deuterium from hydrogen isotope mixtures in a simulated industrial process.
## hydrogen isotope, separation, breakthrough experiment, MOF-74
Citation: Si Y, Wang W, El-sayed ESM, Yuan D. Use of breakthrough experiment to evaluate the performance of hydrogen isotope separation for metal-organic frameworks M-MOF-74 (M=Co, Ni, Mg, Zn). Sc China Chem, 2020, 63, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-020-9722-2
## 1 Introduction
Hydrogen is one of the cleanest sources of energy because it produces neither greenhouse-gas emissions nor air pollution, which is widely used in modern industry and emerging energy sources [1–4]. Deuterium is widely applied not only as a neutron moderator for heavy-water nuclear reactors but also as the tracer for medical treatment and detection, and more importantly, it is the main raw material for nuclear fusion reactors [5,6]. With the increase in the severe energy situation, the fusion reactor has become one of the most promising energy systems due to its unique advantages of high energy release and low pollution [7]. Establishment of the international thermonuclear experimental reactor (ITER), which is co-operated by the European Union, Japan, Russia, China, the United States and Korea, is a representative indicator of progress. However, the utilization rate of deuterium fuel in a nuclear fusion reactor is less than $10\%$ [8]. Considering the nuclear fusion reactor’s economic and environmental safety, the unreacted fuel discharged from the plasma chamber must be recovered and separated. Therefore, how to separate deuterium with low energy and high efficiency is the key to its industrial application. However, the physical and chemical properties of hydrogen isotopes are similar, making it difficult to separate from each other [9]. In the recycling of nuclear fuel, low-temperature distillation has been considered to be the best way to separate hydrogen isotopes because distillation is a very mature industrial technology with long-term prospects of industrial-scale application and has been applied in the commercial production of deuterium. However, this large-scale separation process requires to operate a large number of liquefied hydrogen isotope gases at temperatures below $24\mathrm{K}$ , with the disadvantages of more auxiliary processing equipment and higher energy consumption [10]. There is a gap between the cryogenic distillation technology and the need for large-scale and continuous separation of commercial nuclear fusion reactors in the future. So it is necessary to explore a new largescale hydrogen isotope separation method.
One of the most efficient methods to overcome this challenge is physical adsorption, which has the advantages of low energy consumption, simple process and less investment [11,12]. The core step of this approach is to select an excellent adsorbent that can selectively capture deuterium from the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ mixture to achieve concentration and enrichment of deuterium. Alternatively, we focus on metalorganic frameworks (MOFs), of which the structure encompasses metal clusters or metal nodes connected with organic linkers. MOFs exhibit better performances in gas adsorption and small-molecule separation due to their adjustable pore size and the functionally modified pore space [13,14]. Some basic researches on hydrogen isotope separation by MOFs have been conducted, and it has been found that MOFs as hydrogen isotope separation adsorbents have great application prospect [7,15–20]. However, the study of MOFs as a filler of mixed beds for hydrogen isotope separation in a flow separation system is rare because a very high level of selectivity is demanded to separate any single component from the hydrogen isotopes mixture with similar physical and chemical properties [21]. Some mechanisms, such as kinetic quantum sieve (KQS), chemical affinity quantum sieve (CAQS), and synergistic strategy, have been proposed to promote the in-depth study on selectivity adsorption of a single component from hydrogen isotope mixture [19,22–30]. KQS, based on the limited pore size, was first introduced by Beenakker et al. [31] in 1995, which achieves separation owing to the subtle differences in the kinetic diameters of hydrogen and deuterium at the entrance of microporous materials at cryogenic temperature. Nevertheless, the KQS effect is generally evident only at the condition of very low temperature (always below $77\mathrm{K}$ ), and at the same time, the difference between the aperture of the target adsorbent and the diameter of gas molecule is close to its de Broglie wavelength. Moreover, cryogenic temperature increases energy consumption, and the small pore size is not conducive to gas diffusion, which will further increase the separation cost, so there is a certain gap in practical applications. For the CAQS effect based on equilibrium adsorption, which was first reported in 2013, the deuterium is preferentially adsorbed at strong binding sites even at relatively high temperatures. Therefore, in order to simulate the industrial conditions and increase the separating temperature at high flux, we attempt to seek a way to promote the selective adsorption of a single component by using frameworks with open metal sites.
The key to choosing a suitable framework is to find a material that can preferentially adsorb deuterium from a given mixture, which requires a large difference between the adsorption enthalpy of deuterium and hydrogen [32]. In addition, considering the demand for a large number of adsorbents in the industrial process, our intention is to find a framework with high adsorption capacity that can offset part of the cost. The ideal adsorbent should also have high stability, rapid adsorption, and ease of regeneration. Accordingly, we selected the isostructural series M-MOF-74 1 $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{M}{=}\mathrm{Ni}$ , Co, Mg, Zn) as the promising candidates that meet the above criteria. High concentration of open metal sites (OMS) is exposed in the hexagonal channels of the material after activated dehydration, which is conducive to increase the uptake at low pressure [33–35]. Additionally, in the competitive adsorption of hydrogen isotope mixtures, the deuterium is adsorbed preferentially because of its smaller zero point energy and higher adsorption enthalpy [19,28]. Moreover, the large size of about $11\mathrm{~\AA~}$ enables the diffusion process to proceed quickly, resulting in rapid adsorption kinetics [36,37]. By changing the open metal site of this isostructural series, the interaction between the framework and guest molecule can be well adjusted. Above these, M-MOF-74 materials are combined with reasonable regeneration conditions, high stability and low production cost, making them particularly attractive for commercial applications.
Herein we characterize the performance of M-MOF-74 1 $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{0}$ , Ni, Mg, Zn) to separate hydrogen isotope mixture in a dynamic condition of high flux by a real-time lowtemperature breakthrough instrument, which is closer to the actual industrial process. What’s more, this method is of great significance to the development of materials for hydrogen isotope separation by breakthrough experiments, and it is different from the previous studies based on ideal conditions, for example, infrared spectroscopy and cryogenic thermal desorption spectroscopy (TDS) [17,19,28,29]. The comparison of the results of theoretical simulations and experimental breakthrough data for these ordered microporous materials demonstrate in detail that the breakthrough property of M-MOF-74 ( $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M=Co}$ , Ni, Mg) depends on not only their strong binding sites but also the adsorption capacity of hydrogen isotopes and OMS density. Taking these points into consideration, Co-MOF-74, which showed the longest breakthrough time and $\mathbf{D}_{2}$ captured amount, is the most suitable material for hydrogen isotope separation.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
All starting materials and reagents for the synthesis were purchased from commercial sources and used without further purification. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were recorded on a MiniFlex 600 spectrometer (Rigaku, Japan) with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\mathfrak{a}$ radiation in the angular range of $2\theta{=}5^{\circ}{-}50^{\circ}$ at $293\mathrm{K}$ . Low-pressure $_{(<1.1}$ bar) adsorption measurements were performed using automatic volumetric adsorption equipment (Micromeritics, ASAP 2020 PLUS, USA). Dynamic column breakthrough experiments were conducted using a home-built device with a mass spectrometer (PFELFFER VACUUM). Desktop scanning electron microscope images were collected on a Phenom G2 desktop scanning electron microscope (Thermo, USA).
## 2.2 Synthesis of M-MOF-74 ( $\mathbf{M}{=}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{0}$ , Ni, Mg, Zn)
Co-MOF-74, Ni-MOF-74, Mg-MOF-74 and Zn-MOF-74 were synthesized (Scheme 1) according to the modified method of the previously published literature [33,38–41].
## 2.2.1 Synthesis of Co-MOF-74
$\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\bullet}6H_{2}O}$ $(0.357\ \mathrm{g},$ 1.225 mmol) and $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ (2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid, $0.0723\ \mathrm{g}.$ , $0.365\mathrm{mmol}_{,}^{\cdot}$ were dissolved in a mixture of $10\mathrm{mL}$ of $N{,}N.$ -dimethylformamide, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of water in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ beaker. The mixture was ultrasonicated until homogeneity and then divided into 12 glass tubes. Each tube was frozen with liquid nitrogen and then vacuumed with oil pump and sealed with flame at the same time. After cooling at room temperature, the tubes were transferred to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven. After $24\mathrm{h}$ , purplish-red crystals were obtained.
## 2.2.2 Synthesis of Ni-MOF-74
An identical procedure was used as for Ni-MOF-74 as above, except that $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\bullet}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was used instead of $\mathrm{Co(NO_{3})_{2}\bullet6H_{2}O}$ . Finally, a palm green microcrystalline powder was obtained.
## 2.2.3 Synthesis of Mg-MOF-74
$\mathrm{Mg(NO_{3})_{2}\bullet6H_{2}O}$ $(0.475\ \mathrm{g}$ , $1.853\mathrm{mmol}$ ) and $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ $(0.112\mathrm{g},0.565\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in $45~\mathrm{mL}$ of $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylformamide, $3\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of water in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ beaker. The mixture was ultrasonicated until homogeneity and then divided into 20 glass tubes. Each tube was frozen with liquid nitrogen and then vacuumed with oil pump and sealed with flame at the same time. After cooling at room temperature, the tubes were transferred to $125^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $8\mathrm{h}$ . Then, a yellow microcrystalline powder was obtained. Note that once the heating time was more than $8\mathrm{h}$ , cube bulk impurities would come out, which can be seen in the desktop scanning electron microscope images (Figure S3, Supporting Information online).
## 2.2.4 Synthesis of Zn-MOF-74
$Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}\bullet6H_{2}O}}$ 1 $\mathrm{1.00~g}$ , $3.362\mathrm{mmol}_{.}$ and $\mathrm{H_{4}D O B D C}$ $(0.250\mathrm{~g},1.163\mathrm{~mmol})$ ) were dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of $N{,}N{}$ -dimethylformamide and $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a $100~\mathrm{mL}$ beaker. The mixture was ultrasonicated until homogeneity and then divided into 20 glass tubes. Each tube was frozen with liquid nitrogen and then vacuumed with oil pump and sealed with flame at the same time. After cooling at room temperature, the tubes were transferred to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $20\mathrm{h}$ . Then, yellow crystals were formed at the bottom of the tubes.
## 2.3 Single-component equilibrium adsorption isotherms
Single-component gas equilibrium adsorption isotherms were measured at pressures ranging from 0 to $105\mathrm{kPa}$ using automatic volumetric adsorption equipment, which usages a volumetric method to decide gas adsorption capacity at equilibrium pressure. Prior to gas sorption measurements, all as-synthesized materials were degassed under dynamic vacuum $(\leq10\upmu\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{Hg})$ at a heating rate of $4\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C/m i n}$ to $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ after solvent exchange with methanol six times over three days. For all gas equilibrium adsorption isotherms, warm and cool free-space were measured utilizing ultra-high purity He $(>99.9999\%)$ . The specific surface areas of MMOF-74 were determined by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) model from the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherm data between the relative pressure of 0.05 and 0.3 at $77\mathrm{K}$ . Pure component adsorption isotherms of hydrogen and deuterium at 77 and $87\mathrm{K}$ were collected in liquid nitrogen and liquid argon baths, respectively. After each isotherm measurement, samples were reactivated at a heating rate of $4\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C/m i n}$ until $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under dynamic vacuum for $30\mathrm{min}$ .
## 2.4 Isotherm fitting
Single-component gas equilibrium adsorption isotherms were fitted with Double-Site Langmuir (DSL) model that best describes over the full pressure range, given by the following equation:
$$
N=A_{1}\frac{b_{1}P}{1+b_{1}P}+A_{2}\frac{b_{2}P}{1+b_{2}P}
$$
where $N$ represents the adsorbed amount per mass of adsorbent in $\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g},A_{1}$ and $\textstyle A_{2}$ are the adsorption saturation capacity for site 1 and site $2{\mathrm{~in~mmol/g}}$ , respectively. $b_{1}$ and $b_{2}$ are dimensionless Langmuir parameters for site 1 and site 2, and $P$ represents the pressure of the bulk at equilibrium with the adsorbed phase in $\mathbf{kPa}$ . The fitted curves are presented in Figures S5–S8.
## 2.5 Isosteric enthalpy of adsorption
The isosteric enthalpy of adsorption is calculated from single-component gas equilibrium adsorption isotherms at different temperatures according to the Virial Method:
$$
\ln(P)=\ln(N)+\left({\frac{1}{T}}\right)\sum_{i=0}^{m}a_{i}\times N^{i}+\sum_{j=0}^{n}b_{j}\times N^{j}
$$
where, $P$ is the pressure in $\mathrm{mmHg},N$ is the amount adsorbed in $\mathrm{mg/g}$ , $T$ is the temperature in $\mathrm{\DeltaK}$ , $a_{i}$ and $b_{j}$ represents virial coefficients, and $m$ and $n$ specify the number of coefficients needed for a precise description of the isotherms. The values of the virial coefficients $a_{0}$ to $\textstyle{a_{m}}$ were further used to determine the isosteric enthalpy of adsorption according to the following equation:
$$
Q_{\mathrm{st}}=-R\times\sum_{i=0}^{m}a_{i}\times N^{i}
$$
where $\mathcal{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}$ is the coverage-dependent isosteric enthalpy of adsorption in $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ and $R$ is universal gas constant with the value of $8.314~\mathrm{J~mol}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ .
## 2.6 Ideal adsorbed solution theory
Ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) of Myers and Prausnitz [42] was used to predict the selectivity of gas mixtures from pure component isotherm. Fitting the single-component isotherm with a Dual-Site Langmuir model (Eq. (1)), the binary adsorption selectivity of component 1 over component 2 in a mixture containing 1 and 2 can be defined by Eq. (4):
$$
S=\frac{n_{1}/n_{2}}{P_{1}/P_{2}}
$$
where $S$ represents the ideal selectivity of component 1 over component 2, $n_{2}$ and $n_{2}$ are the molar loadings in the adsorbed phase in equilibrium with the bulk gas phase with partial pressures $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$ .
## 2.7 Dynamic column breakthrough experiments
Dynamic column breakthrough device was a real-cryogenic apparatus with a mass spectrometer as detector (Scheme S1, Supporting Information online). The internal diameter and length of the column were 0.2 and $11\ \mathrm{cm}$ , respectively. The gas flow rate at the inlet was controlled by a mass flow controller (MFC), and the compositions of the outlet gas were continuously monitored by mass spectrometry. The particle sizes of Co-MOF-74 and Zn-MOF-74 were relatively large, so they were dispersed by ultrasonic to be transformed into fine uniform particles before being filled into the column. The particle sizes of Ni-MOF-74 and $\mathbf{Mg}.$ - MOF-74 were small enough without a need for the ultrasonication step. After that, the sample was evacuated with an oil pump for at least $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to remove solvent molecules and get dry powder, and then transferred into the packed column in the glove box. In order to prevent sample escaping from the stainless-steel column, silica wool (about $20\:\mathrm{mg}$ ) was filled into both ends of the column. The packed column was firstly degassed under dynamic vacuum by the oil pump at $403\mathrm{~K~}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ , which made the sample maintain tight and activated completely, then heated with a heater band under flowing Ne $(10\mathrm{mL/min})$ at $403\mathrm{K}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . To guarantee the precision of the breakthrough experiments, both the activated adsorption column and pre-cooling line must be placed in liquid nitrogen or argon tank for approximately $20\mathrm{min}$ prior to measurements. Note that the three-way valve of 3 should be opened to the condition of air at least $5\mathrm{min}$ before tests to keep the flow rate of the mixed gas stable. Different mixed gas flowed through the packed column at different flow rate of $15~\mathrm{mL/min}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ (1/1/98, $\mathrm{vol\%}$ ), $10~\mathrm{{mL/min}}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ $(10/10/80,\mathrm{vol\%})$ and $2\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ (50/50, $v0l\%$ ). Moreover, the sample was regenerated with a Neon flow ( $\mathrm{10~mL/min)}$ at $403\mathrm{~K~}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ .
## 2.8 Adsorption capacity calculation based on breakthrough data
From mass balance across the whole packed column (inputoutput-amount in the free space of the column and the pipeline $:=:$ accumulation), following equations can be obtained [43–45]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\displaystyle Q=M_{\mathrm{max}}-M_{\mathrm{c}}}}\\ {{\displaystyle M_{\mathrm{max}}=a_{i}\ast\left(\tau-\overset\ast{\underset{0}{\iint}}f(\tau){\mathrm d}\tau\right)}}\\ {{\tau=\frac{t^{\ast}q}{{V_{m}}^{\ast}m}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}$ (unit: $\mathrm{mmol/g})$ ) represents the amount of the component $i$ adsorbed by the adsorbent in the column, $M_{\mathrm{max}}$ (unit: $\mathrm{mmol/g)}$ is the amount of component $i$ retained on the column at a particular specific injection amount $\tau(\mathrm{unit:mmol/g})$ at equilibrium, $M_{\mathrm{c}}$ (unit: $\mathrm{mmol/g)}$ is the amount of component $i$ residing in the free space of the pipeline and the column, $t$ (unit: min) is the time that the mixture passes through the packed column, $q$ (unit: $\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min})$ is the flow of inlet gas, $m$ (unit: g) is the quality of activated sample packed with the sample column, $a_{i}$ is the percent of component $i$ in the mixture, $V_{\mathrm{m}}{=}22.4\ \mathrm{L/mol}$ .
## 3 Results and discussion
The materials M-MOF-74 $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M=Co}$ , Ni, Mg and Zn) are synthesized purely, as can be seen from Figure 1(a) in which powder X-ray diffraction patterns (PXRD) are in good agreement with the simulated PXRD of M-MOF-74. BET and Langmuir surface areas of these MOFs are measured by the nitrogen adsorption isotherms at $77\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 1(b)) and detailed values are listed in Table 1. M-MOF-74 exhibits large hexagonal one-dimensional channels with a diameter of $11\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which provide a large window to ensure that hydrogen isotope molecules enter the pores smoothly without the presence of diffusion barriers.
## 3.1 Gas adsorption isotherms and isosteric heat of adsorption
Measuring the gas adsorption isotherm at a given temperature is a standard method for characterizing how a molecule interacts with a material. Pure ${\bf D}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas equilibrium adsorption isotherms for M-MOF-74 $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}_{}0$ , Ni, $\mathbf{Mg}$ , Zn) were measured at 77 and $87\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 2). In all cases, the adsorbed amount of deuterium is higher than that of hydrogen across the pressure range from 0 to $105\mathrm{kPa}$ , implying appreciable quantum effects. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf D}_{2}$ equilibrium adsorption isotherms exhibit fully reversible type I isotherms because of their large channel diameters $(\sim11\mathrm{\AA})$ , which do not limit the diffusion of hydrogen isotope. In the isotherms of Co-, Ni-, and Mg-MOF-74, a steep initial uptake is observed at pressure below $3\mathrm{kPa}$ followed by a gradual rise at higher pressure. Open metal sites, as primary adsorption sites, are first occupied by hydrogen isotope molecules, which was confirmed by the inelastic neutron powder diffraction experiments [46]. Among these frameworks, MgMOF-74 exhibits the highest capacity for deuterium and hydrogen adsorption, followed by Co-, Ni-, and Zn-MOF-74. However, Ni-MOF-74 and Co-MOF-74 show significantly higher adsorbed amount of deuterium $(2.63\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ for NiMOF-74; $2.16\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for Co-MOF-74) and hydrogen $(1.33\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for Ni-MOF-74; $0.80\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for Co-MOF74) at a relevant partial pressure of $0.01\mathrm{kPa}$ . These are substantially higher than those of Mg-MOF-74 $\left(\mathrm{D}_{2}\right.$ : $1.57\mathrm{mmol/g}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ : $0.51\mathrm{mmol/g})$ or Zn-MOF-74 1 $\mathrm{(D}_{2}$ : $.05\mathrm{mmol/g},\mathrm{H}_{2}\colon0.03\mathrm{mmol/g})$ .
In the case of the raw adsorption data for deuterium and hydrogen in M-MOF-74, it is possible to evaluate relative binding strength for each gas based on the virial equation (Figures S9–S13). The difference of isosteric heats of adsorption $(\varrho_{\mathrm{st}})$ for M-MOF-74 is shown in Figure 3 and compared at zero coverage. For M-MOF-74, the principle interaction with hydrogen and deuterium is Coulomb attraction, where OMS play a major role [34]. In all cases, all four compounds display higher initial adsorption heats of ${\bf D}_{2}$ than that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from zero coverage to saturated adsorption. Among these frameworks, deuterium binds most strongly to Ni-MOF-74, Co-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74, with adsorption enthalpies of 13.7, 13.1 and $12.8\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , respectively, and the values of $Q_{\mathrm{st}}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ with the same magnitudes ordering as Ni $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ : $12.1\mathrm{kJ/mol}\mathrm{\Omega}$ ) $>\mathrm{Co}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\colon11.5\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol})>\mathrm{Mg}$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)$ : $11.3\mathrm{kJ/mol})>\mathrm{Zn}$ $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\colon8.3\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol}^{\prime}$ , which reflects that NiMOF-74 has the strongest binding sites with hydrogen isotopes, followed by Co-MOF-74, $\mathbf{Mg}$ -MOF-74 and Zn-MOF74. It is worth noting that the adsorption enthalpies of hydrogen and deuterium obtained in different papers are slightly different because calculation methods are not the same, but these values are reasonable, and the detailed comparison lies in Table S1 (Supporting Information online). As a matter of experience, the phenomenon, that the differences of deuterium and hydrogen initial adsorption heats for Ni-, Co-, and Mg-MOF-74 are greater than $1\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , is rare, indicating preferential deuterium adsorption and significant effect of hydrogen isotope.
## 3.2 Ideal selectivities
The easiest method to determine the preferred adsorption of ${\bf D}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is to divide the adsorbed amount of ${\bf D}_{2}$ by that of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(n\mathbf{D}_{2}/n\mathbf{H}_{2})$ . However, the data given in this way only represent the enhanced adsorption of ${\bf D}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which only be used to simply predict the separation performance of the material but does not indicate the actual selectivity of the gas mixture. Herein the values of $n\mathrm{D}_{2}/n\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were concluded by the simulated function values that were obtained by fitting the pure deuterium and hydrogen adsorption isotherms at the same temperature with the Double-Site Langmuir equation. The advantage of this method is that the $P$ (pressure) as a function of $n$ (adsorption capacity) in the expression can be freely determined. Even if $P$ is minimal, this method is applicable. As shown in Figure 4, the $n\mathrm{D}_{2}/n\mathrm{H}_{2}$ values for Ni-, Co-, Mg-, and Zn-MOF-74 are closed to 4.8, 3.5, 3.5, and 1.7 under almost zero coverage at $77\mathrm{K}$ , which are much larger than that of other types of porous materials (such as porous carbon, zeolite, COFs, etc.) whose $n\mathrm{D}_{2}/n\mathrm{H}_{2}$ values are about 1.06–1.1 [8,23,47–49]. With the increase of pressure, the value of $n\mathrm{D}_{2}/n\mathrm{H}_{2}$ decreases rapidly and finally remains around 1.1. The adsorbed amount of ${\bf D}_{2}$ on M-MOF-74 is larger than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ under the same conditions with the previously reported experiments on carbon materials, zeolite and COFs, which shows that M-MOF-74 materials exhibit better property of preferred adsorption of ${\bf D}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ compared to other porous materials.
Ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) predicts the selectivity of gas mixtures from single-component isotherms. Previous researches on hydrogen isotope mixtures have already applied this theory and proved that it is applicable to estimate hydrogen isotope selectivity of MOFs. Figure 4 (solid lines) shows the $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity (for a 1:1 hydrogen isotope mixture) of M-MOF-74, among which Ni-MOF-74 shows the highest selectivity of 5 at $77\mathrm{K}$ and its selectivity exceeds 2.5 even the pressure approached $100\mathrm{kPa}$ This value is consistent with the selectivity obtained by FitzGerald et al. [19]. For Co- and Mg-MOF-74, the selectivity of hydrogen isotope separation is larger than 2 in all pressure range of $0{-}100\mathrm{kPa}$ . These large selectivities are mainly due to strong interaction between the open metal sites and deuterium or hydrogen. For these materials, the $\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ selectivity pattern is consistent with the adsorption heat and $n\mathrm{D}_{2}/n\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Notably, since the adsorption isotherms of hydrogen and deuterium on Ni-MOF-74, Co-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74 are steep at low pressure, there are some uncertainties in the single-component adsorption isotherm fitting.
## 3.3 Dynamic column breakthrough experiments
To objectively investigate the separation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ mixture for different frameworks of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Mg}^{-}$ and Zn-MOF-74 in practical applications, it is important to ensure that they are optimally synthesized and fully activated prior to measurement. Furthermore, for the breakthrough test, the filling state of the sample in the packed column is also important. And we explored these aspects and tested in the best conditions. Herein dynamic breakthrough experiments were performed with binary $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ $(50/50,\mathrm{vol\%})$ , ternary $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/$ Ne (10/10/80, vol%) and ternary $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ (1/1/98, $\mathrm{vol\%}$ mixtures under 77 and $87\mathrm{K}$ to explore the influence of (1) hydrogen isotope concentration and (2) temperature on the breakthrough properties of M-MOF-74.
Figure 5(a) shows the breakthrough experiments of MMOF-74, in which a mixture $(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne},1/1/98,\mathrm{vol}\%)$ flowed over the column filled with activated sample at $77\mathrm{K}$ . As expected, efficient separation of ${\bf D}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was realized. Especially, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ firstly eluted through the bed while ${\bf D}_{2}$ was still trapped in the adsorbent, affording pure $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Surprisingly, Co-MOF-74 exhibits the longest retention time for deuterium, and the value reached $300\mathrm{min}/\mathrm{g}$ , far exceeding the breakthrough time on Mg-MOF-74 $230\mathrm{min}/\mathrm{g})$ , Ni-MOF-74 $(205\mathrm{min}/\mathrm{g})$ , and Zn-MOF-74 $(10\mathrm{min/g})$ . Herein the ${\bf D}_{2}$ adsorption capacity on Co-MOF-74 $(4.7\mathrm{mmol/g})$ , MgMOF-74 $\left(4.4\mathrm{mmol/g}\right)$ and Ni-MOF-74 $(3.7\mathrm{mmol/g})$ , calculated from the breakthrough curves, was far beyond the value of Zn-MOF-74. Meanwhile, a similar trend of the breakthrough time on M-MOF-74 exhibits for the mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ (10/10/80, vol%) (Figure 5(b)). Furthermore, hydrogen isotope separation was also studied using high concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ (50/50, vol%) mixture, and the results show that deuterium also can be isolated in the packed column (Figure 5(c)). Among these materials, Co-MOF-74 also has the longest retention time of $25\mathrm{min}/\mathrm{g}$ , followed by NiMOF-74 $(17\mathrm{min}/\mathrm{g})$ and Mg-MOF-74 $(10\mathrm{min/g)}$ , while ZnMOF-74 cannot separate this mixture. The amount of deuterium enriched from the equimolar $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ mixture was up to $5.9\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for Co-MOF-74, $5.5\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for Mg-MOF-74, and $4.8\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for Ni-MOF-74, respectively. Comparing the breakthrough times of three mixtures, we can see that the trend of the breakthrough time on M-MOF-74 for the mixed gas $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}\left(50/50,\mathrm{vol}\%\right)$ is different from the results obtained from the mixtures of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}\left(1/1/98,\mathrm{vol}\%\right)$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ $(10/10/80,\mathrm{vol\%})$ . We suspect that the breakthrough time of the material is a result of the combined effect of binding strength, OMS density and adsorption capacity. As outlined above, Ni-MOF-74 possesses the strongest binding site interacting with hydrogen isotopes, while its OMS density and adsorption capacities of deuterium and hydrogen are lower than those of Co-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74. Therefore, for the low concentration mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ (1/1/98, $v0l\%$ ), the retention time of deuterium on Mg-MOF-74 is significantly higher than Ni-MOF-74, where OMS density and adsorption capacity of the framework have a relatively large influence on breakthrough time. As the concentration of hydrogen isotope increase, the adsorption bed with MgMOF-74 retained deuterium for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ $(10/10/80,\mathrm{vol\%})$
mixture is almost the same as Ni-MOF-74, and for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ $(50/50,\mathrm{vol\%})$ mixture with high concentration, the sequence of deuterium breakthrough time of Ni-MOF-74 and $\mathbf{Mg}.$ - MOF-74 is reversed, in which the influence of the binding strength on breakthrough time increases. Because Co-MOF74 has larger adsorption enthalpies of deuterium and hydrogen than that of Mg-MOF-74, and higher hydrogen isotopes adsorption capacities and abundance of OMS than NiMOF-74, it shows the best breakthrough performance of hydrogen isotope separation for all three gas mixtures. Furthermore, we studied the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ separation property using the three gas mixtures by breakthrough experiment at $87\mathrm{K}$ . As shown in Figure 5(d–f), deuterium can also be effectively separated and enriched inside the pores of Co-, $\mathrm{Mg\mathrm{-}}$ , and NiMOF-74 even at higher temperature of $87\mathrm{K}$ , but the retention times of deuterium for all M-MOF-74 are less than $77\mathrm{K}$ . In short, the hydrogen isotope concentration of mixed gas and temperature of the test will affect the breakthrough separation performance of materials with a different degree. All comparable breakthrough phenomena adequately highlight that Co-MOF-74 is the best suitable material in all four M-MOF-74 materials for separating hydrogen isotope, which could be attributed to the synergistic effect of strong binding sites, high OMS density and hydrogen isotopes ad
sorption capacities.
In the actual application of separation, a suitable adsorbent should have excellent regeneration ability and structural stability, so breakthrough experiments of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ (50/50, $v0l\%$ ) mixture on Co-MOF-74, Ni-MOF-74 and $\mathbf{Mg}$ -MOF74 were tested three times to evaluate their cycle performance. As shown in Figure 6, the breakthrough time and captured amount of deuterium scarcely degraded on CoMOF-74, Ni-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74 during three cycling tests. Furthermore, the characteristic porosities and stability of target MOFs were not altered after the breakthrough tests, as demonstrated by nitrogen adsorption curves at $77\mathrm{K}$ and PXRD patterns after breakthrough experiments (Figures S14–S17, S18–S21). The extraordinary repeatability and stability further provide indispensable conditions for the practical application of the materials.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have independently developed a breakthrough device simulating a realistic separation platform for practical applications to explore the challenges of hydrogen isotope separation. Here we verified that Co-MOF-74, as a fixed bed filler in a fluid separation system, not only has good breakthrough performance at high temperatures of 77 and $87\mathrm{K}$ but also exhibits high working capacity, deriving from the combined effect of some positive factors, including strong binding sites, high OMS density, and large hydrogen isotope adsorption capacity. This indicates that the materials with the positive factors mentioned above may be highly competitive in industrial applications. Meanwhile, the results of breakthrough experiments show that the concentration of hydrogen isotope in the mixture and the test temperature will affect the breakthrough performance of the material. For example, the retention time of deuterium on Mg-MOF-74 is higher than Ni-MOF-74 for the mixed gas of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}/\mathrm{Ne}$ (1/1/ 98, $\mathrm{vol\%}$ ). In contrast, the sequence of deuterium breakthrough time of Ni-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74 is reversed for high concentration mixture of $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{D}_{2}$ $(50/50,\ \mathrm{vol\%})$ . Of course, the studies in the field of hydrogen isotope separation by a breakthrough device with MOFs as adsorbents under high flux conditions are still relatively few, so there is still much work that needs to be explored in the future. In addition, this article also confirmed that the breakthrough separation technology, as a gas separation technology with advantages of low cost and low energy consumption, has a broad development prospect to solve the problem of hydrogen isotope separation and provide a direction for the synthesis of hydrogen isotope separation materials in the future.
